module

public import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.SpaceMarkedSplitDeformations
import Sheaf.AlgebraicGeometry.SpaceSquareZeroRingedRealization

/-! # Split flat deformations of algebraic spaces and cotangent Ext

Source: Stacks Project, defos.tex, Tag 0D17; quot.tex, Tag 0D1K;
artin.tex, sections tangent-spaces and inf
(blueprint: lem-sfb-flat-space-split-deformation-ext).
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

set_option autoImplicit false
set_option backward.isDefEq.respectTransparency false
set_option synthInstance.maxSize 4096

universe u w

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits

namespace AlgebraicGeometry.AlgebraicSpace.SplitSpace

/-- Flat marked deformations and trivial-family automorphisms are naturally Ext one
and Ext zero. Their geometric origin, addition, scalar and arbitrary coefficient
base changes are specified below, without imposing finiteness on X or M.
Source: Stacks 0D17, 0GQ5--0GQ8, 08UG; quot.tex, 0D1K; artin.tex, section-inf
(blueprint: lem-sfb-flat-space-split-deformation-ext).

Proof:
1. Write $A_M=K\oplus M$ and $G_M=\mathcal O_X\otimes_K M$. The kernel $J_M=0\oplus M$ of $A_M\to
K$ satisfies $J_M^2=0$, hence its conormal module is $J_M/J_M^2=M$. The pullback of this module to
$X$ is $G_M$, since on a scheme chart the pullback is the sheafification of $\mathcal
O_X(U)\otimes_K M$. Choose a basis $I$ of $M$. Tensoring the basis expansion identifies $G_M$ with
the free module sheaf $\mathcal O_X^{(I)}$, so it is quasi-coherent even for infinite $I$. These
are identities of sheaves, not an assertion that tensor commutes with arbitrary section functors.

2. Given a marked flat deformation $X'_M\to Y_M$, regard its special fibre as $X\hookrightarrow
X'_M$. It is a first-order thickening because it is cut out by the square-zero ideal generated by
$J_M$. The realization lemma identifies its etale site with $X_{\mathrm{et}}$. Apply the conormal
flatness criterion to $(X\hookrightarrow X'_M)\to(\operatorname{Spec}K\hookrightarrow Y_M)$: the
assumed flatness and Cartesian special fibre give the canonical isomorphism
$G_M\xrightarrow{\sim}\ker(\mathcal O_{X'_M}\to\mathcal O_X)$. Restricting scalars along $K\to
A_M$ therefore gives a square-zero extension of $K$-algebra sheaves with specified kernel $G_M$.
An isomorphism of marked deformations respects this kernel identification, since its map on a
generator $a\otimes m$ is determined by its map on $a$ modulo the ideal and its fixed image of
$(0,m)$.

3. Conversely, start with $0\to G_M\xrightarrow{i}\mathcal A\xrightarrow{p}\mathcal O_X\to0$ of
$K$-algebra sheaves with square-zero kernel. The realization lemma, with base
$B=\operatorname{Spec}K$, realizes it as $X\hookrightarrow X'$. Define the base map on ring
sheaves by $(b,m)\mapsto b\,1_{\mathcal A}+i(1\otimes m)$. This is multiplicative because the
kernel is square zero. The morphism clause of the realization lemma gives $X'\to Y_M$. Locally
choose a lift $\widetilde a$ of a section $a$ through the sheaf surjection $p$. Its conormal map
sends $a\otimes m$ to $\widetilde a\,i(1\otimes m)=i(a\otimes m)$, hence is the prescribed
isomorphism. The conormal criterion gives both flatness and the marked Cartesian special fibre.
The same morphism clause identifies extension isomorphisms with marked deformation isomorphisms:
maps fixing $K$ and $G_M$ fix every displayed image of $(b,m)$. The forward and reverse
constructions recover each other's structure maps and kernel markings.

4. Apply the sheaf-algebra extension classification to $f:X\to\operatorname{Spec}K$ and
coefficient $G_M$. Steps 2 and 3 identify its extension isomorphism classes with
$\operatorname{Def}_X(M)$, giving the displayed Ext-one bijection. The split extension $\mathcal
O_X\oplus G_M$ has the base action of step 3 and is the structure sheaf of $X\times_KY_M$ on the
common etale site (on a lifted chart it is $B\otimes_K A_M=B\oplus(B\otimes_KM)$). Its class is
zero by the classification lemma. Thus this bijection uses the stipulated trivial origin, rather
than an unspecified choice in a torsor.

5. For the split extension, a geometric automorphism over $Y_M$ reducing to the identity on $X$
fixes its kernel: $a\otimes m$ is the product of a lift of $a$ and the image of $(0,m)$, and
changing the lift has zero product with the kernel. Conversely an extension automorphism fixes the
base action $(b,m)\mapsto b+i(1\otimes m)$, so the realization lemma gives a unique geometric
automorphism over $Y_M$. The extension classification identifies these automorphisms with
$\operatorname{Ext}^0(\mathrm{NL}_{X/K},G_M)$. Explicitly they are $\alpha_d(a,g)=(a,g+d(a))$ for
$K$-derivations $d:\mathcal O_X\to G_M$. Then $\alpha_d\alpha_e=\alpha_{d+e}$, so the
contravariance of structure sheaves makes no difference to the asserted additive group
identification.

6. Let $u:M\to N$ be $K$-linear and put $v=1\otimes u:G_M\to G_N$. On sheaf-algebra extensions its
coefficient pushout is $P=(\mathcal A\oplus G_N)/\{(i(g),-v(g)):g\in G_M\}$, sheafifying this
quotient; multiplication before quotient is $(a,h)(a',h')=(aa',p(a)h'+p(a')h)$. Its kernel is
$G_N$ and its quotient is $\mathcal O_X$. This is exactly base change along $A_M\to A_N$: the
mutually inverse ring maps are $a\otimes(b,n)\mapsto[(ba,p(a)\otimes n)]$ and $[(a,h)]\mapsto
a\otimes1+\iota_N(h)$, where $\iota_N(c\otimes n)=\widetilde c\otimes(0,n)$. Lifts of $c$ are
chosen locally through the sheaf surjection $p$; the formulas glue because the latter expression
is independent of the lift: $(i(g)\otimes1)(1\otimes(0,n))=0$, because $i(g)$ is locally a sum of
multiples of images of $(0,m)$ and $u(m)n=0$ in $A_N$. The balancing relations give precisely
$(i(g),-v(g))=0$. These generator formulas commute with restriction, hence give the asserted sheaf
identity and the geometric base change by the morphism clause of realization. The conormal map for
$X\subset X_P$ over $\operatorname{Spec}K\subset Y_N$ sends $c\otimes n$ to $\widetilde
c[(0,1\otimes n)]=[(0,c\otimes n)]$, the identity on $G_N$. Thus the conormal criterion applies
with the original flat map $X\to\operatorname{Spec}K$ and proves flatness and the Cartesian
special fibre.

7. By coefficient naturality in the extension classification, the pushout of step 6 has class
$v_*[\mathcal A]$ in Ext one. The derivation automorphism $\alpha_d$ pushes out to $\alpha_{v\circ
d}$, which is the same coefficient map on Ext zero. This proves the stated functoriality for all
$u$, including noninjective maps and the zero map. Taking $u=a\,\mathrm{id}_M$ proves the scalar
formula: the base ring map is exactly $(b,m)\mapsto(b,am)$ and the coefficient map is
multiplication by $a$.

8. For two deformations with sheaves $\mathcal A_1,\mathcal A_2$, their fibre product of rings
$\mathcal F=\mathcal A_1\times_{\mathcal O_X}\mathcal A_2$ is an extension by $G_M\oplus
G_M=G_{M\oplus M}$. Its base action sends $(b,m_1,m_2)$ to $(b+i_1(1\otimes m_1),b+i_2(1\otimes
m_2))$. Realization applies since its kernel $G_{M\oplus M}$ is quasi-coherent by step 1. The
conormal map sends $c\otimes(m_1,m_2)$ to $(i_1(c\otimes m_1),i_2(c\otimes m_2))$, the prescribed
kernel identification. The conormal criterion, with the original flat map
$X\to\operatorname{Spec}K$, therefore proves flatness and the Cartesian special fibre over
$Y_{M\oplus M}$. The two projections of ring sheaves give the two closed inclusions of the given
deformations. This is their gluing along $X$: given compatible morphisms to a target $T$, let
$g:X\to T$ be their common restriction. Their ring maps on $X_{\mathrm{et}}$ combine uniquely to
$g^{-1}\mathcal O_T\to\mathcal F$. Apply the morphism clause of realization with source thickening
$X\subset X_{\mathcal F}$ and target the identity thickening $T\subset T$, over $K$. It gives the
unique map $X_{\mathcal F}\to T$ with the specified restrictions. The identical calculation for
the base is $A_M\times_K A_M=A_{M\oplus M}$; its spectrum is the asserted base pushout (a map to a
scheme factors through an affine neighborhood of the common image of its sole point). Pulling back
along the summation map of coefficient spaces is, by step 6, pushout of $\mathcal F$ along
$G_M\oplus G_M\to G_M$, $(g_1,g_2)\mapsto g_1+g_2$.

9. The construction in step 8 is exactly the Baer sum in the extension classification, hence
corresponds to addition in Ext one. Together with step 7 and the zero class in step 4, this proves
the vector-space assertion: all vector-space identities follow by applying the bijection to the
corresponding identities in Ext. For automorphisms, step 5 gives composition as addition and step
7 gives the required scalar operations and naturality, so it gives the Ext-zero vector-space
identification as well. All uses of geometric reconstruction required only quasi-coherence of
$G_M$ (proved in step 1), and all uses of the conormal criterion required only the original
flatness of $X/K$. No properness, finiteness of $M$, dimension bound, or finite-presentation
hypothesis has entered.
-/
theorem flat_space_split_deformation_ext {K : Type u} [Field K]
    (X : AlgebraicSpace (Spec (.of K))) [HasDerivedCategory.{w} X.SmallModules]
    (hflat : MapIsFlat (structureMap X)) :
    ∃ (deformation : ∀ M : ModuleCat.{u} K, Classes X M ≃ Ext X M 1)
      (automorphism : ∀ M : ModuleCat.{u} K, AutZero X M ≃ Ext X M 0),
      (∀ M : ModuleCat.{u} K, ∃ D : Marked X M, IsTrivial X D) ∧
      (∀ (M : ModuleCat.{u} K) (D : Marked X M),
        IsTrivial X D → deformation M (classOf X D) = 0) ∧
      (∀ (M N : ModuleCat.{u} K) (h : M ⟶ N) (D : Marked X M),
        (∃ E : Marked X N, PullsBack X h D E) ∧
        ∀ E : Marked X N, PullsBack X h D E →
          deformation N (classOf X E) = extMap X h 1 (deformation M (classOf X D))) ∧
      (∀ (M : ModuleCat.{u} K) (a : K) (D E : Marked X M),
        PullsBack X (ModuleCat.ofHom (a • (LinearMap.id : M →ₗ[K] M))) D E →
          deformation M (classOf X E) =
            extMap X (ModuleCat.ofHom (a • (LinearMap.id : M →ₗ[K] M))) 1
              (deformation M (classOf X D))) ∧
      (∀ (M : ModuleCat.{u} K) (D E : Marked X M),
        (∃ G : Marked X (ModuleCat.of K (M × M)), IsGluing X D E G) ∧
        (∀ G H : Marked X (ModuleCat.of K (M × M)),
          IsGluing X D E G → IsGluing X D E H → Related X G H) ∧
        ∀ G : Marked X (ModuleCat.of K (M × M)), IsGluing X D E G →
          (∃ T : Marked X M, PullsBack X
            (ModuleCat.ofHom (LinearMap.fst K M M + LinearMap.snd K M M)) G T) ∧
          ∀ T : Marked X M, PullsBack X
            (ModuleCat.ofHom (LinearMap.fst K M M + LinearMap.snd K M M)) G T →
              deformation M (classOf X T) =
                deformation M (classOf X D) + deformation M (classOf X E)) ∧
      (∀ M : ModuleCat.{u} K,
        (∃ g : AutZero X M, g.val = Iso.refl _) ∧
        ∀ g : AutZero X M, g.val = Iso.refl _ → automorphism M g = 0) ∧
      (∀ (M : ModuleCat.{u} K) (g h : AutZero X M),
        (∃ t : AutZero X M, t.val = g.val ≪≫ h.val) ∧
        ∀ t : AutZero X M, t.val = g.val ≪≫ h.val →
          automorphism M t = automorphism M g + automorphism M h) ∧
      (∀ (M N : ModuleCat.{u} K) (h : M ⟶ N) (g : AutZero X M),
        (∃ t : AutZero X N, AutPullsBack X h g t) ∧
        ∀ t : AutZero X N, AutPullsBack X h g t →
          automorphism N t = extMap X h 0 (automorphism M g)) ∧
      (∀ (M : ModuleCat.{u} K) (a : K) (g t : AutZero X M),
        AutPullsBack X (ModuleCat.ofHom (a • (LinearMap.id : M →ₗ[K] M))) g t →
          automorphism M t =
            extMap X (ModuleCat.ofHom (a • (LinearMap.id : M →ₗ[K] M))) 0
              (automorphism M g)) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry.AlgebraicSpace.SplitSpace
